The command SCTP_CMD_ASSOC_SHKEY (which generates the association shared key) is placed after starting the T1_COOKIE timer and changing state to COOKIE_ECHOED. If sctp_auth_asoc_init_active_key() fails during execution of SCTP_CMD_ASSOC_SHKEY, `asoc->shkey` remains NULL. However, SCTP_CMD_PEER_INIT has already set `asoc->peer.auth_capable` and `asoc->peer.peer_chunks`, allowing the queueing of a DATA chunk with `auth=1` and `shkey=NULL`. Because the T1_COOKIE timer is already started, it may later enqueue a COOKIE_ECHO chunk. When `sctp_outq_flush_data()` attempts to transmit the DATA chunk together with the COOKIE_ECHO, `sctp_packet_bundle_auth()` dereferences the NULL `shkey`, causing a null-pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
sctp_add_cmd_sf(commands, SCTP_CMD_PEER_INIT, SCTP_PEER_INIT(initchunk));
sctp_add_cmd_sf(commands, SCTP_CMD_ASSOC_SHKEY, SCTP_NULL());
...
sctp_add_cmd_sf(commands, SCTP_CMD_TIMER_START, SCTP_TO(SCTP_EVENT_TIMEOUT_T1_COOKIE));
sctp_add_cmd_sf(commands, SCTP_CMD_NEW_STATE, SCTP_STATE(SCTP_STATE_COOKIE_ECHOED));
</text>
Reason:
Moving SCTP_CMD_ASSOC_SHKEY to immediately after SCTP_CMD_PEER_INIT ensures that the shared key generation occurs before the T1_COOKIE timer is started and before the state is changed to COOKIE_ECHOED. If the key generation fails, command interpretation stops (due to the failure), so neither the timer is started nor the state is changed. As a result, no COOKIE_ECHO is enqueued, and the T1_INIT timer (still running) can retransmit the INIT, allowing the client another chance to process the INIT_ACK and retry key setup. This prevents any DATA chunk with a NULL `shkey` from being transmitted, avoiding the null-pointer dereference.

